(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-gi-i-ii-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Gastrointestinal Tract I & II — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 3)","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The GI tract forms a continuous tube from mouth to anus measuring approximately:","choices":["3 meters (about 10 feet)","9–10 meters (about 30 feet)","15 meters (about 45 feet)","1 meter (about 3 feet)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The GI tract is a continuous muscular tube roughly 9–10 meters (about 30 feet) long, running from the mouth to the anus. — Slide 8 (General Structure)"},{"q":"The accessory digestive organs assist the GI tract but develop as outgrowths connected to it, sometimes by ducts. Which group lists only accessory organs?","choices":["Duodenum, jejunum, ileum, and colon, forming the mid-to-distal tube","Esophagus, stomach, small intestine, and large intestine, the tube itself","Pharynx, esophagus, cecum, and rectum, segments of the alimentary canal","Teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, pancreas"],"answer":3,"exp":"The accessory organs — teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas — assist digestion but do not form the continuous GI tube itself. — Slide 9 (Accessory digestive organs)"},{"q":"The peritoneal cavity is divided into two spaces known as the:","choices":["Pleural and pericardial sacs","Supracolic and infracolic compartments only","Greater sac and lesser sac","Right and left paracolic gutters"],"answer":2,"exp":"The peritoneal cavity consists of the greater sac and the lesser sac (omental bursa), connected via the epiploic foramen (of Winslow). — Slide 13 (Peritoneum)"},{"q":"The greater omentum extends from which structure and drapes over the transverse colon and small bowel?","choices":["The lesser curvature of the stomach","The root of the small-bowel mesentery","The porta hepatis of the liver","The greater curvature of the stomach"],"answer":3,"exp":"The greater omentum extends from the greater curvature of the stomach and hangs down like an apron, draping over the transverse colon and small bowel. — Slide 19 (Greater Omentum)"},{"q":"The enteric nervous system controls coordinated gut smooth-muscle activity via how many intramuscular plexuses extending along the gut?","choices":["Two","One","Three","Four"],"answer":0,"exp":"The enteric nervous system coordinates gut smooth-muscle activity through two intramuscular (intramural) plexuses — the myenteric and submucosal plexuses — that run the length of the gut. — Slide 12 (Special Features)"},{"q":"The GI tract is innervated primarily by which division of the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["Sympathetic","None — it is avascular and aneural","Somatic motor","Parasympathetic"],"answer":3,"exp":"The GI tract is innervated primarily by the parasympathetic division (largely via the vagus and pelvic splanchnic nerves), working together with its intrinsic enteric nervous system. — Slide 12 (Innervation)"},{"q":"Which sequence lists stomach regions from superior to the pyloric end?","choices":["Cardia → fundus → body → pyloric region","Fundus → cardia → pyloric → body","Body → fundus → cardia → pylorus","Pylorus → body → fundus → cardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"From superior to the pyloric end, the stomach regions run cardia → fundus (most superior) → body → pyloric region (antrum, canal, sphincter). — Slide 54 (The Stomach)"},{"q":"With respect to the peritoneum, the stomach is:","choices":["Retroperitoneal","Intraperitoneal","Subperitoneal","Extraperitoneal"],"answer":1,"exp":"The stomach is an intraperitoneal organ, suspended by the greater and lesser omenta, with an interior lined by rugae, a gastric canal, pits, and glands. — Slide 58 (Stomach Interior)"},{"q":"The spleen is located intraperitoneally and lies anterior to which ribs?","choices":["Ribs 4–6","Ribs 1–3","Ribs 9–11","Ribs 11–12 only"],"answer":2,"exp":"The spleen lies intraperitoneally in the left upper quadrant, anterior to ribs 9–11. — Slide 136 (The Spleen)"},{"q":"Within the spleen, the component responsible for filtering blood is the:","choices":["Red pulp","White pulp","Portal triad","Splenic hilum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The red pulp is the component of the spleen responsible for filtering blood and supporting hematopoiesis. — Slide 136 (The Spleen)"},{"q":"Which segment of the small intestine is the primary site of chemical digestion and nutrient absorption and makes up ~two-fifths of its length?","choices":["Jejunum","Duodenum","Ileum","Cecum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The jejunum is the middle segment of the small intestine (~2.5 m, about two-fifths of its length) and is the primary site of chemical digestion and nutrient absorption. — Slide 61 (Small Intestine)"},{"q":"The permanent transverse folds of the small-intestinal lining that do NOT disappear when it fills are called:","choices":["Gastric rugae","Colonic haustra","Plicae circulares","Taeniae coli bands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds of the small intestinal wall that do not disappear when the intestine fills. — Slide 63 (Plicae)"},{"q":"The three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle characteristic of the colon are the:","choices":["Omental appendices","Haustra","Taeniae coli","Plicae circulares"],"answer":2,"exp":"The taeniae coli are three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle characteristic of the colon; their tone puckers the wall into haustra, and both are absent in the rectum and appendix. — Slide 91 (Anatomic specializations)"},{"q":"Which portion of the colon is the most mobile and is suspended by the transverse mesocolon?","choices":["Ascending colon","Rectum","Descending colon","Transverse colon"],"answer":3,"exp":"The transverse colon is the most mobile, intraperitoneal, and longest segment, suspended by the transverse mesocolon. — Slide 94 (Transverse Colon)"},{"q":"The pancreatic head is cradled by the C-shaped curve of which adjacent organ?","choices":["The stomach","The spleen","The duodenum","The transverse colon"],"answer":2,"exp":"The retroperitoneal pancreatic head, together with its uncinate process, nestles within the C-shaped curve of the duodenum. — Slides 66, 130 (Duodenum / Pancreas)"},{"q":"The endocrine tissue of the pancreas, comprising only about 1% of pancreatic cells, is the:","choices":["Acinar cells","Uncinate process","Main pancreatic duct","Pancreatic islets"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pancreatic islets are the scattered endocrine tissue of the pancreas, making up only about 1% of pancreatic cells. — Slide 132 (Pancreatic Islets)"},{"q":"The quadrate lobe of the liver is bounded by the gallbladder and which structure?","choices":["The ligamentum teres","The ligamentum venosum","The inferior vena cava","The falciform ligament"],"answer":0,"exp":"The quadrate lobe of the liver lies between the gallbladder and the ligamentum teres (round ligament). — Slide 115 (Lobes of the Liver)"},{"q":"The liver is completely covered by a connective-tissue capsule known as:","choices":["Gerota's fascia","Bowman's capsule","Glisson's capsule","The tunica albuginea"],"answer":2,"exp":"The liver is completely covered by Glisson's capsule, a connective-tissue capsule, and only incompletely covered by peritoneum, leaving a bare area. — Slide 104 (The Liver)"},{"q":"Within the hepatic portal triad, which three structures travel together?","choices":["Portal vein, central vein, and hepatic vein","Portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct","Hepatic artery, cystic duct, and IVC","Splenic vein, SMA, and common bile duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"The hepatic portal triad — found at the corners of a liver lobule — consists of a branch of the portal vein, a branch of the hepatic artery, and a bile duct. — Slide 121 (Histology of the liver)"},{"q":"Of the liver's triple blood supply, the portal vein delivers:","choices":["Blood needing metabolic processing","Oxygenated blood for tissue metabolism","Outflow to the right atrium","Bile from the gallbladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"Of the liver's triple blood supply, the portal vein delivers inflow of blood that needs metabolic processing, having already passed through the GI tract, spleen, and pancreas. — Slide 108 (Liver triple blood supply)"},{"q":"The neck of the gallbladder is continuous with which duct that carries bile toward the common bile duct?","choices":["Main pancreatic duct","Cystic duct","Hepatic portal duct","Accessory duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"The neck of the gallbladder is continuous with the cystic duct, which joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct. — Slide 126 (The Gall Bladder)"},{"q":"The gallbladder sits between which two lobes of the liver?","choices":["Left and caudate lobes","Right and quadrate lobes","Caudate and quadrate lobes","Right and left lobes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gallbladder lies in a fossa between the right lobe and the quadrate lobe of the liver. — Slide 126 (The Gall Bladder)"},{"q":"The celiac trunk arises at the aortic hiatus and gives off all of the following branches EXCEPT the:","choices":["Superior mesenteric artery","Proper splenic artery","Common hepatic artery","Left gastric artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The celiac trunk's three branches are the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries; the superior mesenteric artery is a separate branch that arises directly from the abdominal aorta, not from the celiac trunk. — Slide 139 (Arterial Supply of the Abdomen)"},{"q":"The gastroduodenal and right gastric arteries are branches of which vessel?","choices":["The proper splenic artery","The proper left gastric artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Common/proper hepatic artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"The gastroduodenal and right gastric arteries both arise from the common/proper hepatic artery, downstream of the celiac trunk. — Slide 142 (Common Hepatic Artery)"},{"q":"The inferior vena cava is formed inferiorly by the union of the:","choices":["Bilateral renal veins","Common iliac veins","Paired hepatic veins","Bilateral gonadal veins"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inferior vena cava begins inferiorly with the union of the two common iliac veins, then ascends receiving renal, hepatic, and other tributaries. — Slide 146 (Inferior Vena Cava)"},{"q":"Which of the following drains directly into the inferior vena cava?","choices":["Splenic vein","Superior mesenteric vein","Hepatic veins","Left gastric vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hepatic veins drain directly into the inferior vena cava just below the diaphragm. — Slides 121, 146 (hepatic veins → IVC)"},{"q":"The esophagus has a dual venous drainage that makes it a site of portosystemic anastomosis; it drains into the azygos vein and the:","choices":["Renal vein","Inferior phrenic vein","Gonadal vein","Portal vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Esophageal venous drainage goes to both the azygos system and the portal vein (via the left gastric vein), a clinically important portosystemic anastomosis at the gastroesophageal junction. — Slide 41 (Esophagus)"},{"q":"Abdominal visceral lymph nodes are characteristically arranged around which structures?","choices":["The paired ureters bilaterally","The major arteries (celiac, SMA, IMA)","The peripheral somatic nerves","The extrahepatic bile ducts only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Abdominal visceral lymph nodes are characteristically arranged around the major arteries — the celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, and inferior mesenteric artery — draining ultimately toward the cisterna chyli. — Slide 52 (lymphatic drainage)"},{"q":"The submandibular glands produce approximately what share of total saliva, the largest of the three pairs' contributions?","choices":["About 60–70%","About 25–30%","About 3–5%","About 90%"],"answer":0,"exp":"The submandibular glands produce the largest share of total saliva, approximately 60–70%, more than either the parotid or sublingual glands. — Slides 30–33 (Salivary Glands)"},{"q":"The internal anal sphincter differs from the external anal sphincter in that it is composed of:","choices":["Elastic cartilage tissue, found elsewhere in the body","Skeletal muscle, under voluntary control","Cardiac muscle, found only in the heart","Smooth muscle, not under voluntary control"],"answer":3,"exp":"The internal anal sphincter is composed of smooth muscle and is not under voluntary control, distinguishing it from the external anal sphincter. — Slide 102 (Anal Sphincters)"}]};
